# XSH getcwd()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.getcwd.001
anchor     functions/getcwd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getcwd.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       getcwd(buf, size), with size large enough, returns buf, and buf
           holds a pathname beginning with a slash that stat() resolves to
           the working directory; after chdir("d") it resolves to d.
test       t/001-names-cwd.c

assertion  XSH.getcwd.002
anchor     functions/getcwd.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       After chdir("l/./s/../s"), with l a symbolic link to d, getcwd()
           gives a name of d/s none of whose components is dot or dot-dot
           and none of whose prefixes is a symbolic link (each checked with
           lstat()).
note       "since" is left out: the CHANGE HISTORY does not say when the
           dot and dot-dot part was added (Interpretation 1003.1-2001 #140,
           Issue 7, changed this text).
test       t/002-physical.c

assertion  XSH.getcwd.003
anchor     functions/getcwd.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       After chdir("d//s/"), getcwd() gives a name of d/s with at most
           two leading slashes, no empty component after them and no
           trailing slash.
test       t/003-slashes.c

assertion  XSH.getcwd.004
anchor     functions/getcwd.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       After chdir() to the working directory's own absolute name with
           an extra leading slash, when that names the same directory,
           getcwd() gives a name of it that does not begin with two
           slashes (the single-slash name exists, so it must be chosen).
note       What a pathname beginning with exactly two slashes means is
           implementation-defined (XBD 4.16); where "//" + name fails or
           names another directory the test is NOTINUSE.
test       t/004-single-slash.c

assertion  XSH.getcwd.005
anchor     functions/getcwd.html#ERRORS/ERANGE
anchor     functions/getcwd.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       With n the length of the working directory's name, getcwd(buf,
           n) and getcwd(buf, 1) return a null pointer with ERANGE, and
           getcwd(buf, n + 1) returns buf holding the whole name.
test       t/005-erange.c

assertion  XSH.getcwd.006
anchor     functions/getcwd.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      1988
says       getcwd(buf, 0) with buf not a null pointer returns a null pointer
           with EINVAL.
test       t/006-einval.c

assertion  XSH.getcwd.007
anchor     functions/getcwd.html#ERRORS/EACCES
class      required
since      1988
says       For a process without appropriate privileges whose working
           directory is a/b, with a granting search but not read
           permission, getcwd() either returns a name that resolves (for
           the parent) to a/b or fails with EACCES (the may-fail error for
           this condition).
test       t/007-unreadable-above.c

assertion  XSH.getcwd.008
anchor     functions/getcwd.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      2008
says       With the working directory nested so deep that its absolute name
           is longer than {PATH_MAX} (and pathconf()'s _PC_PATH_MAX), made
           and entered by relative names, getcwd() with a large enough buf
           returns the full name, whose part above the made directories
           resolves to the starting directory.
note       disputed (new): only the RATIONALE says that ENAMETOOLONG is no
           longer allowed here; the normative text lists no such error but
           XSH 2.3 lets implementations add errors unless a page forbids
           them. The test removes its tree itself (by relative names) before
           it reports.
test       t/008-deep.c
